SQL(1)                         P32W Unix                          SQL(1)

NAME
       sql - query and change a SQL database

SYNOPSIS
       sql [-c] [-csv] dbdir [statement ...]

DESCRIPTION
       sql runs SQL statements on the database dbdir, a directory of
       table files made by sqldb(3). With statements (or the commands
       below) as arguments it runs them and stops. Otherwise it reads
       statements from the standard input: at a terminal with the
       prompt sql> (and ...> while a statement goes on over more
       lines), else quietly, as a script.

       A statement ends with a semicolon. A query (SELECT, SHOW,
       DESCRIBE, EXPLAIN) prints its rows in columns under a header
       line (NULL shows as NULL); other statements print nothing, but
       at a terminal INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE tell how many rows they
       changed. An error is printed and the next statement runs; in a
       script the message gives the line where the statement began:

              sql: line 12: no such table: emp

OPTIONS
       -c     make the database (the directory) if it is not there.
       -csv   print rows as comma-separated values: a header line,
              then a line per row; a value with a comma, a quote or a
              newline is quoted, with quotes doubled; NULL is empty.

COMMANDS
       A line starting with a dot, where a statement would start, is
       a command for sql itself:

       .tables           the tables, a name per line
       .schema [table]   CREATE statements that would make the
                         tables (or one) and their indexes again
       .read file        run the statements in a file
       .mode column|csv  print rows in columns, or as with -csv
       .help             a summary of statements and commands
       .quit, .exit      leave (so does the end of the input)

THE LANGUAGE
       Words of the language may be in any case; names of tables,
       indexes and columns too (they are compared ignoring case).
       Table and index names are letters, digits and _, at most 20 of
       them. -- starts a comment to the end of the line, and /* */
       encloses one. 'text' is a string ('' is a quote in it), "name"
       a name that would otherwise be a keyword.

       Values are INTEGER (32 bits), TEXT (at most 1000 bytes in a
       table) or NULL. A column is INTEGER (INT, SMALLINT, BIGINT,
       TINYINT) or TEXT (VARCHAR(n), CHAR(n): at most n bytes), and
       may be PRIMARY KEY (of one column; it is NOT NULL and unique,
       and an INTEGER PRIMARY KEY left out or NULL gets the next
       number), NOT NULL, or UNIQUE.

       CREATE TABLE [IF NOT EXISTS] t (col type [constraints], ...
              [, PRIMARY KEY (col)] [, UNIQUE (col)])
       DROP TABLE [IF EXISTS] t
       CREATE [UNIQUE] INDEX [IF NOT EXISTS] name ON t (col)
       DROP INDEX [IF EXISTS] name
       INSERT INTO t [(col, ...)] VALUES (expr, ...), ...
       UPDATE t SET col = expr, ... [WHERE cond]
       DELETE FROM t [WHERE cond]
       SELECT [DISTINCT] expr [[AS] alias], ... | * | t.*
              [FROM t [[AS] a] [[INNER | LEFT [OUTER]] JOIN u [[AS] b]
              ON cond | , u]] [WHERE cond] [GROUP BY expr, ...
              [HAVING cond]] [ORDER BY expr [ASC | DESC], ...]
              [LIMIT n [OFFSET m]]
       BEGIN, COMMIT (or END), ROLLBACK
       SHOW TABLES, SHOW INDEXES [FROM t], DESCRIBE t
              (SHOW COLUMNS FROM t)
       EXPLAIN SELECT ...
              how the SELECT reads its tables: SCAN (every row) or
              SEARCH ... USING INDEX

       Expressions have + - * / % (on integers; dividing truncates),
       || (joins text), = <> != < <= > >=, AND OR NOT, IS [NOT] NULL,
       [NOT] LIKE (% any characters, _ one; case is ignored),
       [NOT] BETWEEN x AND y, [NOT] IN (list), parentheses, and the
       functions UPPER LOWER LENGTH SUBSTR(s, start [, length])
       ABS COALESCE IFNULL. The aggregates are COUNT(*), COUNT(x),
       SUM, MIN, MAX and AVG (an integer, truncated). ORDER BY may
       name a result column by its number or alias; NULL sorts
       first. LIMIT skip, count is LIMIT count OFFSET skip.

       NULL in an expression makes it NULL, except in IS NULL, AND,
       OR and COALESCE; a WHERE that is NULL is not true. Text that
       is a whole number counts as that number in arithmetic and
       next to an INTEGER; other text sorts after all numbers. An
       overflow of 32 bits and a division by zero are errors.

       A statement that changes the database is done entirely or
       not at all. Between BEGIN and COMMIT the statements are one
       transaction; an error in a statement that had changed
       something rolls it all back. Only one process writes to a
       database at a time; another waits (up to 10 seconds).

EXAMPLES
       sql -c shop "CREATE TABLE item (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
           name TEXT NOT NULL, price INTEGER)"
       sql shop "INSERT INTO item (name, price) VALUES ('pen', 3)"
       sql shop "SELECT name, price FROM item ORDER BY price DESC"
       sql -csv shop "SELECT * FROM item" > item.csv
       sql shop < script.sql         run a script

       At a terminal:

              sql> SELECT d.name, COUNT(*) FROM emp e
                 ...> JOIN dept d ON e.dept = d.id GROUP BY d.name;

FILES
       dbdir/NAME.tbl    a table
       dbdir/NAME.idx    an index
       dbdir/lock        exists while a process writes
       dbdir/journal     what a write changed, to undo it

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every statement worked, 1 if one failed, 2 when the
       database cannot be opened (or for bad arguments).

SEE ALSO
       sqldb(3)

BUGS
       A join has at most two tables, and there are no subqueries,
       no UNION, no views, no ALTER TABLE, no DEFAULT values and no
       floating point. An index and a key are on one column. Space
       left by deleted rows is used again only in part.
